Take the center {F} of the circle, join {AF} and carry it through to the point {G}, and join {FB}. Draw {FH}  from {F} perpendicular to {AB} and carry it through to {K},  join {AK} and {KB}, draw {FL} from {F} perpendicular to {AK}, carry it through to {M}, and join {KN}. [!just III.1; I.12]

Since the circumference {ABCG|arcABCG} equals the circumference {AEDG|arcAEDG}, and in them {ABC|arcABC} equals {AED|arcAED}, therefore the remainder, the circumference {CG|arcCG}, equals the remainder {GD|arcGD}.

But {CD|arcCD} belongs to a pentagon, therefore {CG|arcCG} belongs to a decagon.

And, since {FA} equals {FB}, and {FH} is perpendicular, therefore the angle {AFK} equals the angle {KFB}. [!just I.5, I.26]

Hence the circumference {AK|arcAK} equals {KB|arcKB}.  Therefore the circumference {AB|arcAB} is double the circumference {BK|arcKB}.  Therefore the straight line {AK} is a side of a decagon.  For the same reason {AK|arcAK} is double {KM|arcKM}. [!just III.26]

Now, since the circumference {AB|arcAB} is double the circumference {BK|arcKB}, while the circumference {CD|arcCD} equals the circumference {AB|arcAB}, therefore the circumference {CD|arcCD} is also double the circumference {BK|arcKB}.

But the circumference {CD|arcCD} is also double {CG|arcCG}, therefore the circumference {CG|arcCG} equals the circumference {BK|arcKB}.  But {BK|arcKB} is double {KM|arcKM}, since {KA|arcAK} is so also,  therefore {CG|arcCG} is also double {KM|arcKM}.

But, further, the circumference {CB|arcCB} is also double the circumference {BK|arcKB}, for the circumference {CB|arcCB} equals {BA|arcAB}. Therefore the whole circumference {GB|arcGB} is also double {BM|arcBM}.  Hence the angle {GFB} is double the angle {BFM}. [!just VI.33]

But the angle {GFB} is double the angle {FAB}, for the angle {FAB} equals the angle {ABF|angABF}.  Therefore the angle {BFN|angBFM} equals the angle {FAB}.

But the angle {ABF|angABF} is common to the two triangles {ABF} and {BFN}, therefore the remaining angle {AFB} equals the remaining angle {BNF}. Therefore the triangle {ABF} is equiangular with the triangle {BFN}. [!just I.32]

Therefore, proportionally the straight line {AB} is to {BF} as {FB} is to {BN}.   Therefore the rectangle {AB} by {BN} equals the square on {BF}. [!just VI.4, VI.17]

Again, since {AL} equals {LK}, while {LN} is common and at right angles, therefore the base {KN} equals the base {AN}.  Therefore the angle {LKN} also equals the angle {LAN}. [!just I.4]

But the angle {LAN} equals the angle {KBN}, therefore the angle {LKN} also equals the angle {KBN}.

And the angle at {A|angLAN} is common to the two triangles {AKB} and {AKN}.  Therefore the remaining angle {AKB|angAKB} equals the remaining angle {KNA|angKNA}. [!just I.32]

Therefore the triangle {KBA} is equiangular with the triangle {KNA}. Therefore, proportionally the straight line {BA} is to {AK} as {KA} is to {AN}. [!just VI.1]

Therefore the rectangle {BA} by {AN} equals the square on {AK}. [!just VI.17]

But the rectangle {AB} by {BN} was also proved equal to the square on {BF}, therefore the sum of the rectangle {AB} by {BN} and the rectangle {BA} by {AN}, that is, the square on {BA}, equals the sum of the squares on {BF} and {AK}. [!just II.2]

And {BA} is a side of the pentagon, {BF} of the hexagon, and {AK} of the decagon. [!just IV.15.Cor]

Therefore, *if an equilateral pentagon is inscribed in a circle, then the square on the side of the pentagon equals the sum of the squares on the sides of the hexagon and the decagon inscribed in the same circle.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
